T(0,'# Directional transport discrepancies of the local cubic law in confined rectangular grooves: comparison against 5 nm discrete numerical references')
T(1,'## Abstract')
T(2,'Reduced models of nanoimprint flow commonly use a local cubic law to estimate effective transport through the residual film and grooves. We assess this approximation with lattice Boltzmann calculations of steady, fully filled flow between a planar substrate and a periodically grooved wall.')
T(3,'The period and groove width are 240 and 120 nm. 18 geometries span gaps of 25–125 nm and groove depths of 25–120 nm. Along-groove ({0}) and across-groove ({1}) forcing is applied separately, giving 36 principal-direction samples.')
T(4,'Three results emerge.')
T(5,'**First, the 5 nm discrete results show different depth responses in the two directions.** At a gap of 50 nm, increasing depth from 25 to 120 nm multiplies the along-groove coefficient {0} by 3.55, but the across-groove coefficient {1} by only 1.034. Among the gaps of 50, 75 and 125 nm that cover this depth interval, the relative along-groove gain decreases with gap, but remains approximately 39.2% at 125 nm.')
T(6,'**Second, the local cubic law overestimates transport at all 36 principal-direction samples relative to the 5 nm discrete references.** The overestimates range from +10.30% to +204.85% along the grooves and from +8.11% to +54.19% across them. This is a systematic pattern within the sampled geometries and resolution, rather than a discrepancy confined to a few samples.')
T(7,'**Third, the reference geometry shows discrepancies in both anisotropy and reconstructed flow direction relative to the 5 nm principal values.** At {0} nm and {1} nm, the numerical anisotropy is 2.21, whereas the model gives 3.70, approximately 67% higher. Under the same linear tensor description, the flux directions reconstructed from the two sets of principal values differ by 9.20° for forcing at 45°.')
T(8,'Retrospective evaluation against the 5 nm references, using the published rounded coefficients, gives maximum absolute corrected residuals of 10.5% for the 18 along-groove samples and 18.7% for the 18 across-groove samples. The 15 across-groove samples with {0} have a maximum of 6.9%. At the across-groove sample {1} nm against the 5 nm reference, the signed discrepancy changes from +16.36% to −18.71%, increasing its absolute value. The correction depends only on {2}; the residuals still vary with gap, and their sources have not been separated.')
T(9,'These discrepancy magnitudes and correction residuals are specific to the sampled conditions and 5 nm references; they are neither continuous-solution error bounds nor prediction accuracies for new geometries. Separately, Appendix E uses an energy identity and sectional dissipation inequalities to show that the local cubic law bounds both principal conductances from above in the ideal Stokes model of fixed, fully filled, no-slip periodic straight grooves. This result determines neither the discrepancy magnitudes nor the sign of the anisotropy discrepancy. The quantitative contribution is the sampled directional comparison and the limits of retrospective correction. Model assessment must report {0}, {1}, their ratio and the scope of the numerical reference evidence together.')
T(10,'**Keywords:** nanoimprint lithography; rectangular grooves; anisotropic transport; lattice Boltzmann method; local cubic law; model discrepancy')
T(12,'## 1 Introduction')
T(13,'### 1.1 Motivation')
T(14,'During nanoimprinting, liquid redistributes between a substrate and a patterned mold. Resolving every groove over a large imprint field is impractical. Engineering models therefore use effective transport coefficients, replacing a patterned region with an equivalent uniform gap that carries the same flow under the same pressure difference.')
T(15,'For parallel grooves, a common construction is the local cubic law. Each local height is treated as an independent parallel-plate channel with conductance {0}; deep and shallow regions are combined by an arithmetic average along the grooves and a harmonic average across them. This construction requires only geometry and no fitted parameters.')
T(16,'The construction does not explicitly resolve transverse velocity coupling near sidewalls or flow through the connections between deep and shallow regions. Its overall transport accuracy requires direct assessment when groove depth is comparable to groove width and a connected liquid layer remains beneath the ribs.')
T(17,'### 1.2 Relation to previous work')
T(18,'Effective transport coefficients for unresolved patterns are established in imprint modeling. Jain et al. used anisotropic transport for flow beneath patterned templates, and subsequent models retained this treatment [1–3]. The average-flow model of Patir and Cheng provides a flow-factor framework whose stripe averages are among the relations examined here [4].')
T(19,'Effective transport tensors in confined grooves have also been studied analytically and numerically. Dewangan and Datta combined spectral analysis with domain perturbation to establish tensor relations and evaluate representative topographies [5]. The same authors studied along-groove flow between a sinusoidal wall and a planar wall. Their full spectral method does not assume small amplitude; separate asymptotic approximations have their own limiting conditions [6]. Groove-induced transport anisotropy is therefore not itself a new finding.')
T(20,'We compare the magnitudes and directional differences of local-cubic-law discrepancies against explicitly resolved numerical principal values, and examine the remaining residuals of an empirical correction. The ideal-Stokes upper bounds are established separately in Appendix E. Positive discrepancy alone is not treated as sufficient evidence of novelty. The comparison uses 5 nm discrete references and informs assessment of a reduced model; it does not establish process-prediction accuracy.')
T(21,'### 1.3 Scope of this study')
T(22,'For rectangular grooves of fixed period and width, we apply along-groove and across-groove forcing separately. The principal values obtained on a 5 nm grid serve as discrete references for the local cubic law under common geometry, flux definitions and fluid properties. We retrospectively evaluate the V2 correction. Refinements at four geometries characterize reference sensitivity, rather than certifying continuous-solution accuracy across the map.')
T(23,'The study concerns fixed gaps, fully filled Newtonian liquid and no-slip walls. **Free surfaces, wetting, trapped gas, real-resist rheology and moving molds are outside its scope.** These require separate models and evidence.')
