T(143,'## 5 Discussion')
T(144,'### 5.1 Limits of local-plate averaging and candidate mechanisms')
T(145,'All sampled principal directions show positive discrepancy relative to the discrete references, with different magnitudes in the two directions. The local cubic law therefore overestimates flow capacity under the checked conditions. Global coefficients alone cannot identify which part of the flow dominates the discrepancy.')
T(146,'The along-groove construction averages deep- and shallow-region parallel-plate conductances arithmetically, without resolving transverse viscous coupling or sidewall velocity changes. Sidewall shear and momentum exchange between regions are candidates for further investigation. Their flux or dissipation contributions have not been quantified; the present data do not establish a single drag mechanism explaining the entire overestimate.')
T(147,'The across-groove construction combines local plate resistances in one-dimensional series, while the resolved flow also traverses step connections. Velocity redistribution and viscous dissipation there may affect total resistance. No independent local resistance fractions were measured, and dominance by the narrowest region has not been proved.')
T(148,'Parameter trends also require qualification. In sampled depth sequences at fixed gap, along-groove discrepancy increases with {0}. Including the 25 nm gap, however, removes a monotonically decreasing gap dependence at some depths. At {1} nm, the discrepancy rises from 139.14% at {2} nm to 154.24% at {3} nm. A general rule that a particular mechanism strengthens as the gap narrows is therefore not established.')
T(149,'These mechanisms require an independent dissipation decomposition or reference solutions under matching applicability conditions. Related squeeze-flow work on rectangular line gratings reports limitations of simple lubrication models [11], but its loading, filling state and comparison baseline differ. Its force ratios cannot directly explain the present fixed-gap coefficient discrepancies.')
T(150,'### 5.2 Implications for reduced squeeze-flow models')
T(151,'Fixed-gap coefficients are candidate local inputs to a reduced squeeze-flow model. For a square field at one instant, assume spatially uniform principal coefficients, constant viscosity, prescribed closure speed {0}, principal-axis coordinates and specified edge pressure. Depth-integrated mass conservation gives')
T(153,'Coordinates {0} and {1} run along and across the grooves. At common geometry, closure speed and constant ambient edge pressure {2}, multiplying both coefficients by the same factor divides the overpressure {3} and its area-integrated net load by that factor. Different directional factors generally also change the pressure distribution. Both principal-value and anisotropy discrepancies may therefore affect reduced predictions. This is a conditional model implication; dynamic squeeze flow has not been validated.')
